                "entry_id": "50c87361-b205-3db0-a389-88a9590d4183",
                "word": "neurogenre",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "144b2673-2b5e-4cd1-8a16-aa0d38d7e9c2",
                        "entry_id": "50c87361-b205-3db0-a389-88a9590d4183",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "neurogenres"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "9a788218-3ead-3c17-a009-57350ed77a9e",
                        "entry_id": "50c87361-b205-3db0-a389-88a9590d4183",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Dont l’identité de genre est influencée et/ou liée au neuroatypisme et/ou à la neurodivergence."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Genres humains et identités de genre",
                            "Non-binarité"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "58f0eb9e-f6b0-4808-9118-ea1d1997c3ce",
                                "sense_id": "9a788218-3ead-3c17-a009-57350ed77a9e",
                                "text": "Pour être plus précis, je suis neurogenre. C’est à dire que ma vision du monde et mon genre sont influencés par mon autisme et par le fait que je fais des crises psychotiques. Comprendre la distinction « fille-garçon », c’est difficile pour moi. Quand je pense à moi, je pense surtout que je suis personne autiste. Parce que ça résume bien mes intérêts, ce que j’aime porter, ce que j’aime faire, la manière dont je sociabilise. Donc à mes yeux, le mot « neurogenre » définit beaucoup mieux mon genre que « fille », « garçon » ou même des termes comme « agenre » ou « genderfluid »."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f2cd3127-47e3-411c-92c6-9c64705c22f2",
                                "sense_id": "9a788218-3ead-3c17-a009-57350ed77a9e",
                                "text": "Au cas où quelqu'un aurait besoin de l'entendre : je soutiens pleinement les personnes xenogenres, neurogenre et leurs transitions quelles qu'elles soient."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "1fe9efe2-0499-49d7-9a95-1249e8e224bf",
                        "source_entry_id": "50c87361-b205-3db0-a389-88a9590d4183",
                        "source_sense_id": "9a788218-3ead-3c17-a009-57350ed77a9e",
                        "target_lang_word": "neurogender",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "neurogender",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "41e7cd6a-4435-3d7d-aa69-d0e299c1121c",
                                        "entry_id": "1c44fe5f-43ca-3c1f-a453-8af2ca4da6d3",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The gender of one's brain."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7d86bb57-c54d-4cd3-a008-c2af84e07b73",
                                                "sense_id": "41e7cd6a-4435-3d7d-aa69-d0e299c1121c",
                                                "text": "Murphy was concerned with the “cross gendered,” those whose “neurogender [gender/sex of the brain] is not the same as the morphological/physical/genital gender."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "ea97e073-6a28-380f-a087-91d91f4e1c9a",
                                        "entry_id": "1c44fe5f-43ca-3c1f-a453-8af2ca4da6d3",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A gender type that is linked to neurological factors."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "159275cb-4388-407f-8fbf-10d3ba7da774",
                                                "sense_id": "ea97e073-6a28-380f-a087-91d91f4e1c9a",
                                                "text": "Includes “genderqueer/gender non-conforming”, “genderfluid”, “agender”, “nonbinary”, and other (eg, “neurogender”, “bigender”, “androgyne”, “two-spirit”, “questioning”)"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "71d66021-e32d-4f7c-8117-fa0502a44558",
                                                "sense_id": "ea97e073-6a28-380f-a087-91d91f4e1c9a",
                                                "text": "[page 82:] Neurogenders are genders specific to neurodivergent people whose experience of gender relates to their neurotype […]\n[page 84:] […] So, back to me and neurogenders.\n[page 86:] If you know someone else who is neurogender, or another gender you've never heard of, or simply autistic plus trans of any kind, believe them when they tell you who they are."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "neurogender",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4f4a6e13-fc9a-330c-8ef5-bec5e50c739a",
                                        "entry_id": "c0265525-730f-3526-9a34-684c7705bea7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Having a gender identity linked to one's neurodivergence (particularly autism)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "neologism"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "32e20e02-a8ab-4665-9b03-aa46d42c94d9",
                                                "sense_id": "4f4a6e13-fc9a-330c-8ef5-bec5e50c739a",
                                                "text": "If you know someone else who is neurogender, or another gender you've never heard of, or simply autistic plus trans of any kind, believe them when they tell you who they are."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "22aa9cb7-63aa-490e-a755-49ccc788e696",
                                                "sense_id": "4f4a6e13-fc9a-330c-8ef5-bec5e50c739a",
                                                "text": "Neurogender and autigender people are often met with anger and prejudice, even by some T/GE people who view such new forms of gender identification as threatening to trans respectability."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
            {
                "entry_id": "cc5ea123-b483-3955-8751-c5c97be31311",
